bNJ(a){var s=a==="ar",r=s?"simple, friendly Egyptian colloquial Arabic (\u0627\u0644\u0639\u0627\u0645\u064a\u0629 \u0627\u0644\u0645\u0635\u0631\u064a\u0629)":"simple, friendly English",q=s?', every time it appears, in headings, explanation, summary and boxes alike: the explanation around it is Egyptian Arabic, but the term itself is NEVER replaced by an Arabic translation, because the exam and the slides use the English word. Give the meaning in brackets the first time only: "\u0627\u0644\u0640 Variance (\u0627\u0644\u062a\u0628\u0627\u064a\u0646) \u0628\u062a\u0642\u064a\u0633...", and after that just "\u0627\u0644\u0640 Variance". Write "\u0627\u0644\u0640 Mean" and "\u0627\u0644\u0640 Z-score", never "\u0627\u0644\u0645\u062a\u0648\u0633\u0637 \u0627\u0644\u062d\u0633\u0627\u0628\u064a" or "\u0627\u0644\u062f\u0631\u062c\u0629 \u0627\u0644\u0645\u0639\u064a\u0627\u0631\u064a\u0629" alone. If the material itself is in Arabic, keep its own terms.':"",p=s?"Start every Arabic line with Arabic words (not with an English term) so the line reads right-to-left; put English terms in the middle or end of the sentence. A line that is fully English (a definition quoted from the slides) is fine on its own line.":"Keep Arabic out of English sentences unless the source has it."
return"LANGUAGE:\n- Write explanations, summaries and example answers in "+r+".\n  Keep every technical term in English exactly as the lecture writes it"+q+".\n- Quiz questions and their options are always in English.\n- Keep the tone warm and a little funny: now and then (about once per section) add one short playful line in\n  Egyptian Arabic that makes the student smile (a daily-life comparison, a joke about exams or the doctor) next\n  to the explanation or in a tip box, and quiz explanations may open with a short fun cheer. Never put jokes in\n  definitions, laws, formulas, code or the quiz questions themselves, and never let a joke make a fact wrong.\n- "+p+'\n\nSYMBOLS, MATH AND CODE (very important):\n- NEVER use LaTeX or $...$. Write real Unicode symbols: \xac \u2227 \u2228 \u2295 \u2192 \u2194 \u21d2 \u21d4 \u2261 \u2200 \u2203 \u2208 \u2209 \u2286 \u2282 \u222a \u2229 \u2205 \u2264 \u2265 \u2260 \u2248 \xd7 \xf7 \xb7 \xb1 \u221a \u2211 \u221e \u2115 \u2124 \u211a \u211d.\n- Put every formula, logical expression, truth-table row, equation, code, SQL and syntax in a ``` fenced block with a tag\n  (```formula, ```logic, ```python, ```sql ...), or, for a short piece inside a sentence, in `backticks`.\n  Example: "\u0627\u0644\u0646\u0641\u064a \u0628\u062a\u0627\u0639\u0647\u0627 `\xac(p \u2227 q) \u2261 \xacp \u2228 \xacq`".\n- Use Markdown tables (| a | b |) for comparisons and truth tables, followed directly by their explanation.\n\nVISUALS (don\'t forget them \u2014 a picture is understood faster than text):\n- Wherever a picture explains faster (a process or steps, a structure or hierarchy, a tree, a timeline, a cycle,\n  a comparison, a Venn diagram, a graph, a report/document layout, a slide figure), add a small diagram as a\n  ```svg block right next to the text it explains, followed by one caption line starting with "\ud83d\uddbc\ufe0f".\n- Aim for 1-3 useful diagrams per section (none where they add nothing; never pure decoration).\n- SVG rules: one valid standalone <svg> with xmlns and a viewBox around 0 0 360 220 (max width 640); simple shapes,\n  arrows (<path>/<line> with a <marker>) and SHORT labels (font-size 12-16, font-family sans-serif); English terms\n  stay English; fills in mid-tones (#6366F1, #14B8A6, #F59E0B, #E0507A, #0EA5E9) with white or #1F2430 text, a\n  white background rect; no scripts, external images, fonts, CSS @import or foreignObject.\n- If a slide figure is too complex to redraw, describe what it shows in one or two lines instead.\n'},
